 page in  that way as well  and did you find that you discovered um  more about yourself and your family  relationships as you were  were writing the novel  yeah i guess i did like mary the lead  character and  she has a son called tj the book's about  a woman who disappears so she just walks  out of her life as we know it  we don't know where she's going there's  no plan there's no warning  and she leaves behind her 18 year old  son but but i write a lot about  his early years and and those bits were  very um  it was very lovely to write actually and  i guess in writing them i realized that  i knew how to be a mother if you know  what i mean you know in writing  someone else being a mother and that  kind of familiar um  relationship between her and her son  there was a kind of yeah  a comforting realization that maybe i  knew what i was doing  maybe i don't you ever really do no i  don't think you do i can tell you mother  of three that i am you never know what  you're doing  um did you were you able to answer  the question of when you give so much to  to other human beings does that become  your  absolute focus and you you lose yourself  it was the answer yes  yeah the answer was yes um and and  that's one of the journeys that mary has  to take in the book is  she spent her whole life looking  outwards at other people  caring nurturing just helping other  people stay  healthy and stay alive she's never ever  from a young age  really um had the luxury of thinking  about herself she's never really known  that was something she could do so the  book is about that it's about  finally you know a woman finally being  confronted with herself
 

